Page 1 of 35

-- 1 of 35 --

Kept from BCQCI: the resource names, the record and field names ( mix_code ,
mc_quantity , bt_ticket and the rest), the unit and material type codes, and the
request_status reply block.
Changed: each plant computer answers only for itself, so URLs carry no plant code.
JSON is the supported format.
Added: orders, order status, order loads and tickets, which BCQCI 3.4 does not define;
the JSON mix design upload; the error_detail reply field; all-or-nothing writes; the
status ping with result ids for collecting new batch results; and API key authentication.
Tickets work like the ticket command of the Universal Link (ULink) interface.
Not implemented: the BCQCI items listed under Limitations (section 16).
3. Conventions
Must marks a requirement. A request that breaks one is refused, usually with the
reason in error_detail .
Should marks a recommendation.
Field names, resource names and literal values are in code type. <angle brackets>
mark a value the caller supplies.
Codes in examples (plant 101 , order 445676 , mix 3000INT ) are sample values.
4. Transport and methods
Each plant computer runs its own copy of the API and answers only for itself. Software
that serves several plants talks to each one at its own address.
Transport: HTTP on the plant computer's web port (8080 unless the plant was set up
otherwise).
Format: JSON. Every request body and every response is a JSON object.
Version: v1 . It is part of every URL.
Methods: GET reads. PUT updates a record, or creates it if it doesn't exist. POST only
creates. DELETE removes a ticket that hasn't started batching, and nothing else.
Authentication: an API key on every request (section 4.1).
Connection check: GET status/ answers whenever the plant computer's API is
running (section 15.3).
