-- 2 of 35 --

Field names are lower case and case-sensitive. Fields not listed in this document are
ignored.
4.1 Authentication
Every request must carry an API key made at the plant. A request without a valid key gets
401, and nothing is read or written.
Sending the key. Put it in the Authorization header: Authorization: Bearer rk_… .
The header X-API-Key: rk_… is accepted too. The key is case-sensitive: send it
exactly as the plant showed it.
Getting a key. The plant makes and deletes keys on its Settings > API Keys screen.
Each program should have its own key, so one can be deleted without stopping the
others.
Shown once. The plant shows a key only when it is made and keeps only a hash of it. A
lost key can't be recovered: the plant deletes it and makes a new one.
Access. A key is read-only (GET) or read-write (GET, PUT, POST and DELETE). A read-
only key sending anything but GET gets 403 Forbidden .
Format. A key is rk_ followed by 64 lower-case hex digits. Treat it like a password:
keep it out of URLs, logs and source code.
Wrong keys. After 10 failed requests from one address, counting requests with no key,
that address is refused until a minute has passed since its first failure, even with a
good key. A good request doesn't reset the count. Programs behind one proxy or
gateway share its address, and so share its lockout.
Replies. Every 401 and 403 carries an error_detail saying why: no key, a wrong or
deleted key, too many wrong keys, or a read-only key. If the plant database is busy
while the key is checked, the reply is 503 Database is busy.
Encryption. On the plant's local network plain HTTP works. Any connection from
outside it should use HTTPS or a Tailscale connection to the plant, so the key can't be
read in transit.
curl -H "Authorization: Bearer rk_<your key>" http://<plant
address>:8080/rhino_rest_api/v1/json/status/
