PUT /rhino_rest_api/v1/json/orderstatus/
{ "orderstatus": [ { "order": { "order_num": "445676", "status_id": 6 } } ] }
An unknown order returns 404 Record not found. , and nothing in the request is
saved.
POST returns 409; use PUT.
The reply is 201 Updated .
GET orderstatus/<order_num> returns the order with all the fields listed under Orders,
as a one-element array: "orderstatus": [{"order": {...}}] . An unknown order
returns 204 with an empty array, and orderstatus/ with no order number returns 400.
13.2 Order loads
GET orderloads/<order_num> returns the order with all its loads:
{ "request_status": {...},
"order": { "order_num": "445676", "status_id": 4, ...,
"loads": [ { "load": { "ticket": 100050, "load_size": 7, ... } }
] } }
The order fields are the same as under Orders. The load fields are listed under Loads.
Every load of the order is returned. An order with no loads yet returns "loads": [] .
Count the array for the number of loads.
An unknown order returns 204, and orderloads/ with no order number returns 400.
orderloadslist/<order_num> returns the same, with each load as a short listitem .
14. Tickets
A ticket is one load of an order, queued at the plant ready to batch. PUT tickets/ or
tickets/<ticket_num> adds a ticket, or changes it when the plant already has that ticket
number and it hasn't started batching. POST only adds. One request can also create the
order, the truck and the driver.
Tickets work like the ticket command of the Universal Link (ULink) interface. One
message carries a load with its order, customer, truck, driver, extra products and ticket
totals, and the plant queues it ready to batch. The differences: a resent ticket number
changes that ticket instead of adding a second load, unknown products are refused rather
than skipped, and every reply says what the plant did.
