{
"request_status": { "status": 201, "msg": "CREATED", "detail": "Created" },
"tickets": [ { "ticket": { "ticket_num": 100050, "load_id": 1, "order_num":
"445676",
"result": "created", "added_at_plant": ["order 445676", "driver Steve",
"truck 12"] } } ]
}
The reply is 201 Created if the request added at least one ticket, otherwise 201 Updated .
Each ticket's result is created or updated , and added_at_plant lists the order, truck
and driver the request added. Check that list: a typo in a truck number adds a new truck.
14.2 What the plant does with a ticket
The load is queued as Ready (status 3) on the loading screen. The operator still starts
batching.
Sending a ticket number again changes that ticket. Only the fields sent change:
extras or totals left out are kept, and the load keeps its mix, even one changed on
the load screen.
A ticket that has started batching, or was canceled at the plant, can't be changed: 409
Conflicts with what is stored. , and nothing is saved.
A ticket number used before on another order is history if that load is finished, and a
new ticket is made. If that load isn't finished, the reply is 409.
A ticket for an order the plant marked Complete re-opens it (status 4, Loading). A
ticket for a Canceled order gets 409, unless its order block sends a new status_id .
A ticket can't set its order to Complete or Canceled (400). Use orderstatus for that.
An order block can't change the mix of an order that has loads (409 Order already
has loads. ), so a resent ticket never changes its mix.
Two tickets for the same order, or the same ticket twice, in one request are refused
(400). Send them in separate requests.
Drivers. A driver_name becomes the load's driver and the driver of the load's truck,
as when the load screen changes a load's driver. Without one, a ticket moved to
another truck takes that truck's driver, and sending the truck the load is already on
leaves the driver as it is.
New trucks get capacity 12 (or the load size, if larger) and the ticket's driver, else the
load's, else the plant's first driver (normally Unassigned). A plant with no drivers at all
gets a driver named Unassigned, listed in added_at_plant . A truck number that was
Concrete Rhino REST API Specification
